      "text": "We have a polynomial equation $3x^3 - 3x^2 + 11x - 8 = 0$ with roots $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$  By Vieta's formulas, we know that:\n\\begin{align*}\na + b + c &= \\frac{-(-3)}{3} = 1, \\\\\nab + ac + bc &= \\frac{11}{3}, \\\\\nabc &= \\frac{-8}{3}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nWe want to find $ab + ac + bc.$  To do so, we can use the identity:\n\\begin{align*}\n(a + b + c)^2 &= a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + 2(ab + ac + bc).\n\\end{align*}\n\nSubstituting the known values, we have:\n\\begin{align*}\n1^2 &= a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + 2\\left(\\frac{11}{3}\\right) \\\\\n1 &= a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + \\frac{22}{3} \\\\\na^2 + b^2 + c^2 &= 1 - \\frac{22}{3} \\\\\na^2 + b^2 + c^2 &= -\\frac{19}{3}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nNow, we can use the identity:\n\\begin{align*}\na^2 + b^2 + c^2 &= (a + b + c)^2 - 2(ab + ac + bc).\n\\end{align*}\n\nSubstituting the known values, we have:\n\\begin{align*}\n-\\frac{19}{3} &= 1^2 - 2(ab + ac + bc) \\\\\n-\\frac{19}{3} &= 1 - 2(ab + ac + bc) \\\\\n-\\frac{22}{3} &= -2(ab + ac + bc) \\\\\nab + ac + bc &= \\boxed{\\frac{11}{3}}.\n\\end{align*}The answer is: 11/3",
